My research aims to improve the seismic safety, repairability, and resilience of steel buildings and their structural and nonstructural components. The work integrates large-scale testing, refined finite element simulation, theoretical modeling, and performance-based design.

Resilient Steel and Composite Structures
======

I investigate the cyclic behavior, damage development, and repairability of steel and steel-concrete composite structures. Particular attention is given to connections that can reduce local damage, facilitate prefabrication, and support rapid post-earthquake recovery.

Innovative Beam-to-CFST Column Connections
======

My current research develops seismic design methods for irregular steel beam-to-concrete-filled steel tubular column joints, including staggered-floor and unequal-depth beam configurations. Connection concepts under study include inclined or partially inserted diaphragms and concrete-anchored slip-critical blind bolts.

Seismic Retrofit and Post-Earthquake Repair
======

This research evaluates vulnerable welded beam-to-HSS column connections in existing high-rise steel buildings and develops practical retrofit and repair strategies using wing plates, haunches, and local stiffening components. The objective is to redistribute damage, restore strength, and improve structural resilience.

System-Level Seismic and Collapse Assessment
======

I conduct experimental and numerical studies of multi-story steel buildings under severe earthquake loading. This work includes cyclic pushover analysis, collapse assessment, connection-fracture modeling, and interpretation of data from large-scale shake-table tests.

Steel Frame-Curtain Wall Interaction
======

My emerging research examines the coordinated seismic response of steel frames and glass curtain walls. Key topics include drift accommodation, connection deformation, glass-frame interaction, damage evolution, replaceable components, and system-level resilience optimization.

Methods and Capabilities
======

- Full-scale and large-scale cyclic loading tests
- Three-dimensional shake-table testing
- ABAQUS and OpenSees numerical simulation
- Nonlinear material, contact, fracture, and damage modeling
- Component-based mechanical modeling
- Parametric analysis and design-method development
- Multi-source experimental data processing and response interpretation
